# Waste Classification using Transfer Learning (MobileNetV2)

A deep learning project that classifies a photo of a waste item into one of six categories: **cardboard, glass, metal, paper, plastic, trash**. It uses transfer learning with a pretrained **MobileNetV2** (ImageNet weights) and the **TrashNet** dataset.

## Why this project
Waste must be sorted by material before it can be recycled. Manual sorting is slow and error-prone, so this project automates the first step: recognising the type of waste from an image.

## Dataset
- **TrashNet** by Gary Thung and Mindy Yang: https://github.com/garythung/trashnet
- 2,527 images, 6 classes

| Class | Images |
|---|---|
| cardboard | 403 |
| glass | 501 |
| metal | 410 |
| paper | 594 |
| plastic | 482 |
| trash | 137 |

Split: 70% train (1,769), 15% validation (384), 15% test (374).
The dataset is **not** stored in this repository. The notebook downloads it automatically (or uses `dataset-resized.zip` if you place it in the project folder). Please see the TrashNet repository for its licence.

## Model
```
Input (224x224x3)
 -> Data augmentation (horizontal flip, rotation 0.1, zoom 0.2)
 -> MobileNetV2 preprocess_input (scale to [-1, 1])
 -> MobileNetV2 base (ImageNet weights, include_top=False, 154 layers)
 -> GlobalAveragePooling2D
 -> Dropout (0.3)
 -> Dense (6, softmax)
```
- Total parameters: 2,265,670 (8.64 MB)
- **Stage 1:** base frozen, train the new head (Adam, lr = 1e-3, 7,686 trainable parameters)
- **Stage 2:** fine-tune the top 40 base layers (Adam, lr = 1e-5)
- Loss: sparse categorical cross-entropy
- Callbacks: EarlyStopping (patience 3, restore best weights) and ReduceLROnPlateau (factor 0.3, patience 2)

## Results (test set, 374 images)
| | Accuracy |
|---|---|
| Before fine-tuning | 84.76% |
| After fine-tuning | **85.03%** (318 / 374 correct) |

| Class | Precision | Recall | F1 |
|---|---|---|---|
| cardboard | 0.88 | 0.88 | 0.88 |
| glass | 0.87 | 0.90 | 0.89 |
| metal | 0.89 | 0.82 | 0.85 |
| paper | 0.88 | 0.89 | 0.89 |
| plastic | 0.77 | 0.83 | 0.80 |
| trash | 0.70 | 0.58 | 0.64 |

Trash is the weakest class because it has the fewest images (137). Common confusions: metal predicted as glass, glass as plastic, and trash as plastic.

## How to run
**Google Colab (recommended):** upload `Waste_Classification_Transfer_Learning.ipynb`, set the runtime to GPU, and run all cells.

**Locally:**
```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
jupyter notebook Waste_Classification_Transfer_Learning.ipynb
```
Training on a CPU is slower (about 30-60 minutes); a GPU takes a few minutes.

The notebook also saves the trained model as `waste_model.keras` and launches a small **Gradio** demo: upload a waste photo and get the top-3 predicted classes.

## Project files
| File | Description |
|---|---|
| `Waste_Classification_Transfer_Learning.ipynb` | Full pipeline: data, model, training, evaluation, demo |
| `waste_model.keras` | Trained model (if included) |
| `requirements.txt` | Python packages |

## Acknowledgements
- Dataset: [TrashNet](https://github.com/garythung/trashnet) (Gary Thung, Mindy Yang)
- The overall idea was adapted from [aniass/Waste-Classification](https://github.com/aniass/Waste-Classification). This version uses a different dataset (6 classes), a multi-class softmax head, MobileNetV2 preprocessing, 224x224 inputs, global average pooling, two-stage fine-tuning, callbacks and a more detailed evaluation.
